---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';

const examples = [
	'A suitable version of cmake was not found (required v4.3.2).',
	'A suitable version of powershell-core was not found (required v7.5.4).',
].join('\n');
---

<p>
	Before building those libraries, vcpkg checks the tools it uses. It may download its own CMake
	and PowerShell 7 even though <code>cmake --version</code> worked: its package cache depends on
	specific tool versions, and Visual Studio does not install PowerShell 7. Lines like these announce
	normal tool downloads, rather than a failure to configure the build:
</p>
<Code code={examples} lang="text" title="Example output, 13 November 2026" />
<p>
	Those examples came from Visual Studio 2026's bundled vcpkg on 13 November 2026. That run
	downloaded about 53 MB of CMake and 117 MB of PowerShell. Other Visual Studio or vcpkg versions
	can select different tools and print different versions or an order unlike this example. A clean
	Windows Sandbox run that day fetched CMake 4.4.3, PowerShell 7.6.6 and 7-Zip 26.03; a tool's
	downloaded version can exceed the minimum the message names.
</p>
<p>
	Keep vcpkg's default tool selection. You do not need to install PowerShell 7 separately or
	change a setting to force system tools. Let the downloads finish and check for the final
	<code>Configuring done</code> and <code>Generating done</code> lines below. Later configures
	reuse the downloaded tools and built libraries while their caches remain available.
</p>
